"""Focused short-answer variants for questions previously receiving whole lessons."""
def short_answer_blocks(q,a):
 if q['source_set']!='short' or a.get('source_discrepancy'):return None
 text=q['original_prompt'].lower();keys=a.get('modules',[])
 def p(t):return [{'type':'paragraph','text':t}]
 def table(head,rows):return [{'type':'table','headers':head,'rows':rows}]
 if 'caustic' in keys:
  if 'sulphate' in text:return p('Sodium sulphate helps prevent caustic embrittlement by restricting concentrated alkali from entering fine cracks in stressed boiler steel. It is a conditioning measure, not neutralisation of NaOH. The required concentration depends on the boiler-water treatment programme.')
  if 'co2' in text:return p('Dissolved CO₂ forms carbonic acid: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃. This lowers pH and attacks boiler/condensate-line metal. Deaeration and suitable controlled water treatment reduce this corrosion.')
  if 'oxygen' in text:return p('Dissolved oxygen causes corrosion and pitting of boiler metal. Remove it by mechanical deaeration and suitable oxygen scavengers. For sodium sulphite: 2Na₂SO₃ + O₂ → 2Na₂SO₄.')
  return p('Sodium carbonate can produce alkaline conditions, and evaporation concentrates NaOH in crevices and stressed joints. The concentrated alkali promotes intergranular cracking called caustic embrittlement. Prevent it by controlling alkalinity, using phosphate rather than carbonate conditioning, and suitable inhibitors/treatment.')
 if 'coal' in keys:
  if 'highest' in text:return p('Anthracite is the highest-ranking coal. Its approximate calorific value is 8,000–8,500 kcal/kg, depending on grade and the reporting basis.')
  if 'volatile' in text:return p('Volatile matter affects ignition, flame length, smoke and combustion-air requirements. Its determination helps select the furnace size, combustion space and air supply needed for efficient burning of the coal.')
  if 'nitrogen' in text:return p('Nitrogen contributes little useful heat and may form nitrogen oxides during combustion. Low nitrogen content is therefore desirable to reduce heat dilution and NOₓ pollution.')
  if 'ash' in text:return p('Ash is non-combustible: it lowers useful heating value, causes slagging and creates disposal problems. Fixed carbon gives sustained heat and is important for metallurgical coke. Hence good coal should contain low ash and a suitable high fixed-carbon content.')
  if 'moisture' in text:return p('Moisture consumes heat during evaporation, lowers the effective calorific value and increases transport/handling weight. Measuring it helps assess fuel quality and the useful heat obtainable from the coal.')
  return p('Good coal should have high useful calorific value, low moisture, ash and sulphur, and suitable volatile matter and fixed carbon for the intended furnace. Selection also considers availability, cost, combustion behaviour and pollution requirements.')
 if 'energyextras' in keys:
  if 'coal' in text:return p('Coal is burnt in a boiler to produce steam. The steam drives a turbine coupled to an electrical generator; the steam is then condensed and the water returned to the boiler. Coal thus supplies the heat for electricity generation.')
  if 'wind' in text:return p('Wind rotates turbine blades, converting its kinetic energy into shaft rotation. A generator converts the mechanical energy into electricity. Wind is renewable and requires no fuel, but output varies with wind speed and site conditions.')
  if 'radioactive' in text:return p('Radioactive decay releases nuclear energy, mainly as heat. Radioisotope generators convert decay heat to electricity for long-lived remote or space power. Advantages are high energy density and continuous output without sunlight; radiation protection and safe containment are essential.')
  if 'list' in text:return p('Non-conventional energy sources include solar, wind, biomass/biogas, geothermal, tidal and wave energy. They help diversify supply and reduce dependence on exhaustible fossil fuels.')
  return p('Fossil fuels are exhaustible, unevenly distributed and cause CO₂ and other pollutant emissions. Alternative energy sources improve supply security, diversify energy availability and can reduce pollution and dependence on fossil fuels.')
 if 'knocking' in keys:
  if 'ethyl bromide' in text:return p('Ethyl bromide was used as a scavenger with tetraethyl lead. It helps convert lead-containing combustion deposits into more volatile lead bromide, reducing deposits on engine parts. This describes historical leaded petrol, not modern unleaded-fuel practice.')
  if 'tel' in text:return p('Tetraethyl lead was historically added to petrol as an antiknock agent. It suppresses premature autoignition and raises octane quality, improving smooth operation. Its toxic lead emissions led to the adoption of unleaded petrol.')
  if 'arrange' in text:return p('Increasing knocking tendency in a petrol engine: naphthalene < iso-octane < n-octane. Aromatic/branched structures resist knock better than the straight-chain n-octane in this comparison.')
  if 'octane' in text and 'cetane' in text:return p('Octane number measures petrol’s resistance to knocking by comparison with iso-octane (100) and n-heptane (0). Cetane number measures diesel ignition quality; higher cetane means shorter ignition delay. They describe different engine-fuel properties.')
  return p('Knocking in a petrol engine is sudden autoignition of unburnt end-gas, causing pressure waves and a metallic sound. Use suitable high-octane fuel, correct ignition timing, effective cooling and an appropriate compression ratio to reduce it.')
 if 'cracking' in keys:
  if 'regenerat' in text:return p('Coke deposited on a cracking catalyst blocks active sites and lowers activity. Regeneration burns off the coke in air and restores the catalyst for reuse; the recovered heat also supports the cracking process.')
  if 'conditions' in text:return p('The fluidized-bed example operates at about 500°C and 3–5 kg/cm² using finely divided silica–alumina/aluminosilicate catalyst. Vapour suspends the catalyst particles; spent catalyst is regenerated by burning off coke.')
  return p('Cracking converts heavy, high-boiling hydrocarbons into smaller hydrocarbons by heat, catalysts or both. Types: thermal cracking—liquid/vapour phase; catalytic cracking—fixed-bed and fluidized-bed processes.')
 if 'refining' in keys:
  if 'straight' in text:return p('Straight-run petrol is the gasoline fraction obtained directly by fractional distillation of crude petroleum, before cracking or reforming. It is collected in the appropriate gasoline boiling range from the fractionating column.')
  if 'steps' in text:return p('Main steps: remove suspended impurities and water, reduce objectionable sulphur compounds, heat the crude oil, and separate its fractions by fractional distillation. Higher-boiling fractions condense lower in the column; lower-boiling fractions are collected higher up.')
 if 'goodfuel' in keys:
  if 'gaseous' in text:return p('Gaseous fuels mix readily with air, burn with controllable flames, normally leave negligible ash, and permit easy starting/stopping. They reduce manual handling and can give cleaner combustion, but require safe leak control and appropriate storage or pipelines.')
  return p('A good fuel has high calorific value, moderate ignition temperature, low moisture and ash, controllable combustion and low smoke/corrosive impurities. It should be available, economical and convenient to store, transport and handle safely.')
 if 'gasfuels' in keys and 'sources' in text:return p('LPG is obtained from petroleum refining and natural-gas processing. CNG is methane-rich natural gas obtained from gas production/processing, including associated petroleum gas, and compressed for storage and use.')
 if 'batteryclasses' in keys:
  if 'space' in text:return p('Hydrogen–oxygen fuel cells give direct chemical-to-electrical conversion, useful efficiency and continuous output while reactants are supplied. They have quiet operation, and their product water can be useful in spacecraft. Reactant storage and system reliability remain important.')
  return table(['Primary battery','Secondary battery'],[['Intended for single discharge','Can be recharged by reversing reactions'],['Example: dry cell or primary zinc–air','Example: lithium-ion or lead–acid']])
 if 'lithium' in keys and any(word in text for word in ['king','future','why']):return p('Lithium-ion batteries combine high energy density, low self-discharge and useful rechargeable cycle life in a compact, lightweight form. They power phones, laptops, electric vehicles and storage systems. “King/future battery” describes these advantages, not superiority for every application.')
 if 'zincair' in keys and 'applications' in text:return p('Zinc–air batteries are used in hearing aids, small medical devices and other compact portable equipment. They offer high energy density by obtaining oxygen from the surrounding air.')
 if 'methanol' in keys:return p('A methanol–oxygen fuel cell converts chemical energy directly into electricity. Methanol is oxidised at the anode and oxygen reduced at the cathode. Overall: CH₃OH + 3/2 O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. Electrons pass through the external load.')
 if 'stm' in keys and 'afm' in keys:return table(['STM','AFM'],[['Measures tunnelling current','Measures tip–sample force'],['Requires a conducting path','Can examine conductors and insulators'],['Conducting tip with current/height feedback','Cantilever tip with deflection/oscillation detection'],['Constant-current/constant-height modes','Contact/non-contact/tapping modes']])
 if 'biosensor' in keys and 'principle' in text:return p('Glucose oxidase catalyses glucose oxidation, producing hydrogen peroxide. At a suitably biased electrode, H₂O₂ → O₂ + 2H⁺ + 2e⁻. The resulting current is related to glucose concentration by calibration, giving amperometric glucose measurement.')
 if 'spectroscopy' in keys:return p('Spectroscopy studies the interaction of electromagnetic radiation with matter. Beer–Lambert law states A = εlc: absorbance is proportional to concentration at fixed path length within the valid range. For dye analysis, measure standards and the sample at a suitable wavelength and use the calibration graph to determine concentration.')
 if 'ir' in keys:
  if 'active' in text and 'passive' in text:return table(['Active IR','Passive IR'],[['Uses its own infrared source','Detects radiation emitted by objects'],['Measures reflected/transmitted/absorbed radiation','Requires no illuminating source'],['Example: IR absorption gas analyser','Example: thermal night-vision camera']])
  if 'night' in text:return p('Passive thermal cameras detect infrared radiation emitted by warm objects and form images from temperature differences, allowing intruder detection in darkness. Active near-IR systems instead illuminate the scene and detect reflected light. Night vision is an IR detection/imaging application.')
  if 'co detection' in text:return p('An IR source sends radiation through the gas sample. CO absorbs at its characteristic band, reducing the signal at the detector. A reference channel and calibration relate the change to CO concentration. An analyser with its own source is active IR detection.')
 if 'nano' in keys and 'top' in text:return table(['Top-down','Bottom-up'],[['Breaks bulk material into nanosized structures','Builds from atoms, molecules or clusters'],['Ball milling, etching, lithography','Sol–gel, precipitation, CVD'],['May introduce defects/contamination','Requires control of nucleation and growth']])
 if 'lubricants' in keys and 'solid' in text:return p('Two solid lubricants are graphite and molybdenum disulphide (MoS₂). Suitable low-volatility MoS₂ coatings are used for some space/vacuum mechanisms. There is no single universal space-lubricant composition; selection depends on temperature, vacuum and the contacting materials.')
 if 'lubrication' in keys:
  if 'oils' in text:return p('Thick-film lubrication uses an oil with suitable viscosity to maintain a continuous separating film, such as a properly selected mineral or synthetic oil. Thin-film lubrication needs high oiliness and adhesion, provided by fatty oils or suitable boundary/antiwear additives in formulated oils.')
  if 'delicate' in text:return p('Lubrication introduces a material between moving surfaces to reduce friction and wear. Fluid-film lubrication is the course classification for suitably lubricated delicate instruments such as watches, clocks and sewing machines. The actual regime depends on speed, load and clearance. Transformer oil mainly provides insulation and cooling.')
 if 'protection' in keys and 'compare' in text:return table(['Sacrificial anode','Impressed current'],[['Active metal such as Mg/Zn supplies electrons','External DC source supplies electrons'],['No external power; anode is consumed','Needs power, auxiliary anode and monitoring'],['Simpler, limited current output','Adjustable output for larger structures']])
 if 'galvanic' in keys:
  if 'compare' in text:return p('Differential-metal corrosion arises when dissimilar metals contact in an electrolyte; the more active metal is anodic. Differential-aeration corrosion arises from unequal oxygen access, even on one metal; the oxygen-poor area becomes anodic. Both involve anodic dissolution and a coupled cathodic reduction.')
  if 'salt' in text:return p('Under a salt-water drop, the oxygen-poor centre becomes anodic and dissolves, while the oxygen-rich rim is cathodic. Dissolved salts increase conductivity; chloride can also damage passive films. This differential-aeration cell accelerates local corrosion.')
 if 'healing' in keys and 'intrinsic' in text:return table(['Intrinsic','Extrinsic'],[['Repair uses reversible bonds or interactions within the matrix','Repair uses an added healing agent in capsules/channels'],['Example: reversible-bond polymer activated by heat','Example: crack ruptures microcapsules and releases agent'],['Can allow repeated healing under suitable conditions','One-use capsules are depleted; replenished channels can heal repeatedly']])
 return None
